\n  | B(\\mathbb R^d)$. Thus inverse images of open sets under addition are\n  | product-measurable, and the class of sets with product-measurable preimage is\n  | a sigma-algebra containing all open sets, hence contains every Borel set.\n  | Therefore addition is measurable for the product sigma-algebra and\n  | $(s,h)\\mapsto\\mathbf1_{W_n}(s)\\mathbf1_{W_n}(s+h)$ is measurable for Borel\n  | $W_n$. Under AC$_\\omega$ in [A1], these Borel-measurable functions are also\n  | measurable for the product Lebesgue sigma-algebras used in Tonelli. Since $C$\n  | is Lebesgue measurable by $C\\in L^1$, its lift\n  | $(s,h)\\mapsto C(h)$ is measurable for the product Lebesgue sigma-algebra:\n  | the preimage of a Lebesgue-measurable set $E$ is $\\mathbb R^d\\times E$.\n  | Thus $C(h)\\mathbf1_{W_n}(s)\\mathbf1_{W_n}(s+h)$ is product-measurable as\n  | well.\n  | \n  | [F10] [[thm-dominated-convergence]]: pointwise convergence dominated by an\n  | integrable function permits passage of the limit through the integral.\n  | \n  | [F11] [[prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets]]: under the already assumed $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R^d$ is sigma-finite, as witnessed by its increasing finite-measure cubes; its restriction to each bounded measurable $W_n$ is finite.\n  | \n  | \n  | \n  | \n  | **Proof technique:** establish the exact covariance double integral, then use\n  | the Van Hove overlap limit and dominated convergence.\n  | \n  | 1.1 By [F11], Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R^d$ is sigma-finite under the assumed $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Its restriction to $W_n$ is finite by [A1], and the probability measure has total mass $1$. Thus all measure factors used below satisfy the sigma-finiteness hypotheses of [F5] and [F6], including the unbounded lag-space factor in $W_n\\times\\mathbb R^d$. For every $s$, [F4] with $V=1$ gives $\\mathbb E|X(s)|\\le(\\mathbb E[X(s)^2])^{1/2}=M<\\infty$, where $M$ is constant by the stated first- and second-order stationarity. The map $(s,\\omega)\\mapsto |X(s,\\omega)|$ is product-measurable; Tonelli [F5] gives $\\mathbb E\\int_{W_n}|X(s)|\\,ds =\\int_{W_n}\\mathbb E|X(s)|\\,ds\\le M|W_n|<\\infty.$ Therefore the sample path is absolutely integrable almost surely. Tonelli also makes the event where this integral is finite measurable; its probability is one. Applying Tonelli separately to $X^+$ and $X^-$ makes both section integrals measurable, and their difference on that event, extended by zero on its null complement as in [A2], is measurable. [A1, A2, F1, F3, F4, F5, F6, F9, F11, given]\n  | \n  | 1.2 Since $\\int_{W_n}\\mathbb E|X(s)|\\,ds<\\infty$, Fubini [F6] applies to $X$ on $W_n\\times\\Omega$. Its constant mean gives $\\mathbb E[\\bar X_{W_n}]=\\frac1{|W_n|}\\int_{W_n}\\mathbb E[X(s)]\\,ds =\\frac1{|W_n|}\\int_{W_n}\\mu\\,ds=\\mu.$ [A2, F3, F6, F11, given, algebra]\n  | \n  | 1.3 Put $Z(s):=X(s)-\\mu$. The rectangle-preimage argument in [F9] makes $(s,t,\\omega)\\mapsto Z(s,\\omega)Z(t,\\omega)$ product-measurable. By [F4], $\\mathbb E|Z(s)Z(t)|\\le C(0)$ for all $s,t$. Iterated Tonelli [F5] therefore gives a finite integral, at most $C(0)|W_n|^2$, of the absolute pair product over $W_n\\times W_n\\times\\Omega$. Each $\\Omega$-section is integrable by this bound, so Fubini [F6] makes $(s,t)\\mapsto\\mathbb E[Z(s)Z(t)]$ measurable; it equals $C(t-s)$ by [F2]. A second Fubini calculation yields $\\mathbb E\\bigl[(\\bar X_{W_n}-\\mu)^2\\bigr] =\\frac1{|W_n|^2}\\int_{W_n}\\int_{W_n}C(t-s)\\,dt\\,ds.$ This also proves $\\bar X_{W_n}\\in L^2$. [A2, F2, F4, F5, F6, F9, F11, given]\n  | \n  | 1.4 Define $q_n(h):=|W_n\\cap(W_n-h)|/|W_n|$. The addition-measurability argument in [F9] makes $(s,h)\\mapsto\\mathbf1_{W_n}(s)\\mathbf1_{W_n}(s+h)$ product-measurable, so Tonelli [F5] gives measurability of its section integral $q_n$. Also $0\\le q_n(h)\\le1$. For fixed $h$, translation invariance [F7] gives $|W_n\\setminus(W_n-h)|=|(W_n+h)\\setminus W_n|.$ The